    "abstract": "Box Hill, a suburban center in Melbourne with a large Chinese-born population, has changed within a decade from a low-rise shopping strip into a cluster of apartment towers built above shops, services, and public transport. This article defines this form as a vertical ethnoburb: a suburban migrant center in which high-rise housing, everyday services, and transit are concentrated within a single walkable district. The article explains this transformation through four linked conditions: residents’ prior familiarity with apartment living; planning controls that permitted taller buildings near the rail and bus interchange; a finance system in which construction depended on enough apartments being sold before building began; and dense settlement services such as food outlets, tutoring, telecom, finance, migration services, and parcel logistics. The study draws on Australian census data, planning documents, 45 semi-structured interviews, field observations, and site mapping. The findings show that neither migrant demand nor height permission alone explains Box Hill’s vertical growth. Tall apartment clustering emerged where planning permission, presales finance, and settlement services aligned. Residents also described high-rise life through the everyday circuit between apartment, lift, podium, street, and transit, rather than through skyline or height alone.",
